  "prediction": "The Department of Defense (DOD) has policies in place to address unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. However, a review of DOD's policies and practices found that they do not fully align with the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's (CDC) framework for preventing sexual violence. Specifically, DOD's policies on sexual harassment do not include all of the CDC's recommended principles, such as addressing risk factors and providing anonymous reporting mechanisms.\n\nThe report found that DOD's policies on sexual harassment are inconsistent across the military services, and that most existing policies have not been updated to reflect the changed definition of sexual harassment as an adverse behavior that can contribute to a hostile or offensive work environment. The report also found that DOD's data collection efforts on sexual harassment are inadequate, and that the department lacks a centralized system for tracking and reporting incidents of sexual harassment.\n\nThe report makes four recommendations to DOD to improve its policies and practices on sexual harassment, including:\n\n1. Fully incorporating the CDC's framework for preventing sexual violence into DOD's policies on sexual harassment.\n2. Developing a centralized system for tracking and reporting incidents of sexual harassment.\n3. Providing anonymous reporting mechanisms for servicemembers to report incidents of sexual harassment.\n4. Developing a comprehensive strategy to address the root causes of sexual harassment in the military.\n\nThe report also notes that DOD has made progress in addressing unwanted sexual behaviors, including the establishment of a new office to oversee the prevention of sexual assault and harassment. However, more work is needed to ensure that DOD's policies and practices are effective in preventing and responding to these behaviors.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for DOD to take a more comprehensive and coordinated approach to addressing unwanted sexual behaviors, and to prioritize the prevention of these behaviors through education, training, and policy changes.",
